WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:03.638
Two product changes each add thirty dollars in revenue

00:00:04.228 --> 00:00:08.175
But the same cap can preserve one gain and erase the other

00:00:09.228 --> 00:00:13.781
Why cap at all A few big spenders can dominate the difference between

00:00:13.661 --> 00:00:14.902
experimental groups

00:00:15.452 --> 00:00:19.889
Even an unchanged customer can shift that difference simply by landing

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:21.745
in one group rather than the other

00:00:22.757 --> 00:00:26.833
A cap replaces every amount above a threshold with that threshold

00:00:27.462 --> 00:00:31.964
Here a thousand dollars becomes fifty That reduces the influence of

00:00:31.844 --> 00:00:33.059
extreme spending

00:00:34.103 --> 00:00:35.486
But it changes what you measure

00:00:36.264 --> 00:00:40.688
Thirty dollars of growth among ordinary customers survives this cap

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:45.436
The same growth from a customer already above fifty disappears

00:00:46.491 --> 00:00:50.838
The paper asks how to choose a cap without making the final test misleading

00:00:51.436 --> 00:00:55.757
A working belief such as two percent growth for each customer predicts

00:00:55.637 --> 00:00:58.631
which cap best balances signal and noise

00:00:59.228 --> 00:01:02.041
Then the actual experiment supplies the estimate

00:01:03.061 --> 00:01:04.277
The belief can be wrong

00:01:05.145 --> 00:01:09.788
If the selected cap settles toward a fixed value as samples grow standard

00:01:09.668 --> 00:01:13.937
confidence intervals can remain valid for the effect at that cap under

00:01:13.817 --> 00:01:15.071
the paper's conditions

00:01:15.645 --> 00:01:17.543
Stable does not mean optimal

00:01:18.549 --> 00:01:22.006
Choosing whichever cap looks most significant is different

00:01:22.561 --> 00:01:27.102
When there is no effect that can pick the luckiest random fluctuation

00:01:27.686 --> 00:01:31.312
More data alone does not remove this selection problem

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:36.687
In the paper's simulation at one hundred thousand users this rule

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.664
falsely rejects twelve point two percent of the time

00:01:40.228 --> 00:01:45.258
Belief selection rejects five point one percent against a five percent

00:01:45.138 --> 00:01:45.709
target

00:01:46.757 --> 00:01:49.080
These results concern capped revenue

00:01:49.686 --> 00:01:53.170
A reliable test can still miss growth above the cap

00:01:53.757 --> 00:01:58.233
The contribution is to explain when data-driven capping supports valid

00:01:58.113 --> 00:02:02.511
inference and where conclusions about total revenue still need more

00:02:02.404 --> 00:02:02.910
work
